# Pastel Bar

Zebar 3.3.1 on Windows. The 34px hardware, active-window and AI pills each have a transparent web view over a Windows Composition host-backdrop surface. The background and its charcoal tint (#35383D at 153/255, 60% tint opacity; text and icon opacity are unchanged) share a rounded geometry clip with a 17px radius. A fourth Zebar window reserves 52px at the top edge and ignores mouse input. This full-width spacer uses the same host-backdrop blur with a lighter, square-cornered tint (#1E2024 at 110/255), so the pills sit on a frosted strip. The pill windows do not take keyboard focus.

CPU, GPU and RAM show text labels, utilization and a 60-sample history in a 400px hardware pill; per-metric icons are omitted. Hardware is sampled every second; the foreground window updates every 400ms. Percentages use regular-weight text: 14px for hardware and 10px for AI, with fixed-width columns and tabular digits. The Codex icon is white for dark-background contrast.

Each AI window uses one 108×11px rounded rectangular bar: its pastel fill shows **remaining quota**, and its brighter 1.5px outline shows the **time remaining** until reset and shrinks from the right. Fill reaching past the outline means quota is outlasting the cycle. The top and bottom edges extend equally: at 50%, both end at the horizontal midpoint. Reset date/time is inside on the left (local HH:mm for 5h, M/d for weekly), with the remaining percentage on the right, both in regular-weight text. The AI pill is 351px wide, with 16px provider padding, 10px icon-to-bar gaps and 4px between quota rows; provider names are omitted because the brand icons identify Codex and Claude Code. The 320px active-window pill stays at the primary monitor center regardless of Spotify. Overflowing active-window titles use the same scrolling timing as Spotify with the app icon fixed; short titles stay centered and reduced motion uses a static ellipsis. Hardware and the 132px Spotify pill sit to its left; AI and date/weather follow to its right. Every adjacent pair uses one shared gap, up to 56px, reduced equally when either side needs room within the 16px screen margins. At 1920px, all gaps are 56px with or without Spotify; date/weather follows AI at that same gap. Positions use physical monitor coordinates and the spacer DPI scale. If zero gaps are insufficient, the active-window pill shrinks toward 120px, preserving its center. All pills remain visible. Below 1216px with Spotify (1185px without it), at 100% display scaling, these minimum widths cannot fit with 16px margins: the helper sets appearance.layoutConstrained in its snapshot without writing a disconnect-triggering stderr warning, preserves side windows and keeps the active window centered; overlap or overflow may remain at such unsupported widths. Use a wider monitor or lower display scaling. Widths recover automatically when space returns. Spotify keeps the record on the left and stacks the title above the three controls in the remaining second column, with 10px left and 16px right inner padding, a 6px column gap, a 10px title, 24x18px buttons and 14px icons. Overflowing titles pause for 1.2 seconds, scroll left at 22px per second, pause for 1.2 seconds at the end, then reset and repeat even while paused; short titles stay centered, the full-title tooltip remains, and reduced motion uses a static ellipsis. Paused records stop spinning and controls explicitly target the verified Spotify session. Time uses the reported reset and cycle duration (five hours or seven days for older cache entries), updates locally every second, and stays full after expiry until fresh reset data arrives. Missing reset dates show `--`; unknown cycle lengths leave the frame empty. Window labels remain in tooltips and accessibility text. Each window comes from observed quota data. Codex Pro plans omit the 5h gauge. Hover for full reset times and the last observation time.

One PowerShell helper reads OpenCodex caches every two seconds, checking modification times before parsing. When observations are older than five minutes, it makes a read-only quota GET using the existing Codex / Claude login. Failed requests back off and respect Retry-After. After a 401/403, renewed access tokens clear authentication backoff at the next 30-second identity check; old-token authentication or network failures cannot restore that wait. Same-account rate-limit delays remain in force. OAuth tokens stay inside the helper and are never sent to the UI or written to the display cache. Claude's usage endpoint is undocumented; it can change. Usage outside the local proxy is visible at the five-minute refresh interval.

The helper exits when Zebar exits, when the widget requests a stop, or after 20 seconds without a widget heartbeat. A named mutex prevents duplicate quota requests during a reload.

## Install on Windows

Install Zebar with Scoop, then apply the configuration and start all four windows:

```powershell
rtk proxy chezmoi apply "$env:USERPROFILE/.glzr/zebar"
rtk proxy zebar startup
```

The source state is `dot_glzr/zebar` in the dotfiles repository; files are installed under `%USERPROFILE%\.glzr\zebar`. This pack is ignored by chezmoi on non-Windows systems. Runtime `data/`, logs and local settings backups are excluded from source control. Existing Codex and Claude Code logins remain outside the repository; the helper reads them at runtime without refreshing OAuth.

To recreate the current login startup shortcut after installing Zebar through Scoop:

```powershell
$zebarExe = Join-Path $env:USERPROFILE 'scoop/apps/zebar/current/zebar.exe'
$shortcutPath = Join-Path ([Environment]::GetFolderPath('Startup')) 'Zebar.lnk'
$shortcut = (New-Object -ComObject WScript.Shell).CreateShortcut($shortcutPath)
$shortcut.TargetPath = $zebarExe
$shortcut.Arguments = 'startup'
$shortcut.WorkingDirectory = Split-Path $zebarExe
$shortcut.Save()
```

Official icon sources and the local API compatibility adjustment are documented in `assets/SOURCES.md` and `vendor/SOURCE.md`.
